Z||000000||wxxo||03-24-2000||02:33 PM||||whats that all about? cant you just dump it in like a normal car?? then how?? im sure that someone knows. tia||||reg||5
Z||000001||ROB MAC||03-24-2000||03:18 PM||||I was told to open rad cap,set heater switch and temp. to high(fan doesnt need to be on ) open thermostat cap and remove thermostat .Fill at thermostat opening till it overflows at rad then cap the rad. IT helps to have someone at the front to do this.Continue filling then cap the thermostat .Do not replace thermostat yet. Cap should be closed to first notch not fully tightened.Cap arrows (on top of cap) will point to the front and back of car. Run the car till warm .Turn off car , open cap (coolant may gush out) . Top it up and replace thermostat , cap it tight( arrows will point towards thermostat hose )and fill coolant recovery bottle to proper level. Drive a couple of days keeping an eye on temp guage and check level at thermostat cap when COLD and top up if necessary. Worked for me.||||reg||1
Z||000002||ROB MAC||03-24-2000||03:34 PM||||P.S. If you are only topping up do it with engine COLD and remove thermostat and fill as this is the highest point in the system||||reg||1
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||03-25-2000||08:32 PM||||I realize that many people do it many ways that work also but its my 2 pennies! And I have done this way too many times to not offer it.<P>Refill procedures used at Fiero Motorsports, (after a presure test is done on the cooling system.)<P>Take off the radiator cap, thermostat cap and thermostat. Fill thermostat housing until coolant comes out of the radiator. Cap off the radiator. Fill thermostat housing to the top. Once it gets to the top let it sit. (We usually start to clean up and check for any obvious leaks at this point.) The coolant level might start to go down, I usually give it 10 or 15 minutes to go down. If it goes down some more than wait until it doesn't. (If you didn't preasure test the system and you find that the coolant is going into resevoir, replace the radiator cap!) When it doesn't go down anymore than pop in the thermostat, and close it up. At this point I start the car and let it go through its heat cycle(I make sure the AC is NOT on or the heater.) If everything goes well than it should warm up, go to the 220 point, fan will go on, temp will go back down to 185 or 200, fan will go off, one more check for any leaks, top off the resevoir, Fiero is done!<P>I really have to recommend a coolant preasure pump to check for leaks. REALLY great when your car is overheating and you don't know why!<P>Hope this helps someone.||||reg||2
Z||000004||Gokart||03-26-2000||07:40 AM||||Try this: <A HREF="http://home.att.net/~fieroshadow/fierohelp/coolant.html" TARGET=_blank>http://home.att.net/~fieroshadow/fierohelp/coolant.html</A> ||||reg||1
